{
"segments": [
{
"text": "Case One.",
"start": 0.75,
"end": 1.667,
"pause": 0.6
},
{
"text": "The Prize Sponge.",
"start": 2.267,
"end": 3.526,
"pause": 1.4
},
{
"text": "The Tidewhistle Cove Summer Show was the biggest day of the year, and for the first time Agnes Bell had been asked to judge the baking.",
"start": 4.926,
"end": 12.862,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "She took the job very seriously. Every cake arrived first thing in the morning and was set on the long table in the village hall under its own glass dome, with a white cloth draped over the top. Each one had only a number card in front of it. No names, no labels, and no peeking. Agnes kept the entry list, which said who had baked what, folded in her handbag. The bakers themselves had dropped off their cakes at nine and been sent firmly on their way.",
"start": 13.712,
"end": 40.187,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "At noon Agnes went across to the Kettle and Gull, her tea room, for a quick lunch. Three people stayed behind in the hall. Morwenna Day was arranging sweet peas at the far end. Hedley Truscott was stacking chairs. And Jago Penhallow, the baker, was fussing over the bread display, because although his own sponge cake was entry number four, he also had loaves in the bread class.",
"start": 41.037,
"end": 64.247,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "When Agnes came back at one o'clock, entry number seven was gone. The glass dome was there. The cloth was there. The plate was there, with a few crumbs on it. The cake itself had vanished.",
"start": 65.097,
"end": 76.809,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Constable Ollie Penrose arrived within minutes, slightly out of breath from the hill. He gathered the three helpers by the stage.",
"start": 77.659,
"end": 85.275,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "\"Now then,\" he said kindly. \"One of the entries on the baking table has gone missing. Entry number seven. I'm not accusing anybody. I just want to know what you saw.\"",
"start": 86.125,
"end": 96.835,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Morwenna spoke first. \"I was down at the far end with the flowers the whole hour. I did see Hedley walk past the cake table a couple of times with chairs.\"",
"start": 97.685,
"end": 107.413,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "\"I did walk past it,\" said Hedley, rubbing the back of his neck. \"You can't get to the chair cupboard any other way. But I never lifted a cloth. I wouldn't know one cake from another under those things.\"",
"start": 108.263,
"end": 120.209,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Jago folded his floury arms. \"Well, don't look at me. I've got my own entry to worry about. Why on earth would I want somebody else's lemon drizzle?\"",
"start": 121.059,
"end": 130.809,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes looked at him for a long moment. Then she glanced at the empty plate, and at the folded entry list peeking out of her handbag.",
"start": 131.659,
"end": 139.637,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Can you solve it? Who took entry number seven, and how does Agnes know?",
"start": 140.737,
"end": 144.727,
"pause": 1.2
},
{
"text": "Think about it.",
"start": 145.927,
"end": 146.951,
"pause": 0.9
},
{
"text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
"start": 147.851,
"end": 153.077,
"pause": 4.5
},
{
"text": "The Solution.",
"start": 157.577,
"end": 158.601,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Jago Penhallow took the cake.",
"start": 159.701,
"end": 161.557,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Ollie had only said that entry number seven was missing. He never said what kind of cake it was. Every cake sat under a glass dome and a white cloth, so nobody could see it or even smell it, and the only list of what each entry was had been folded in Agnes's handbag all morning.",
"start": 162.407,
"end": 180.263,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The baker of entry seven was Kerensa Hale, and it was indeed a lemon drizzle. The only way Jago could have known that was by lifting the cloth.",
"start": 181.113,
"end": 190.095,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Jago went pink to the tips of his ears. He admitted that he had only meant to peek at the competition, but the cake looked so perfect that he panicked about his own sponge and tucked it into the bread crate in his van. It was returned without a crumb missing, apart from the ones on the plate. Jago withdrew his sponge from the contest, and Kerensa's lemon drizzle won first prize.",
"start": 190.945,
"end": 213.921,
"pause": 0.0
}
],
